 "equations": [],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Charging signatures",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Four charger schemes are covered: BC1.2 dedicated charging port, the Chinese YD/T 1591-2009 standard (a subset of BC1.2), Divider mode and 1.2 V mode. Divider and 1.2 V schemes are used by particular phone and tablet makers."
  },
  {
   "topic": "BC1.2 detection",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "In primary detection the portable device drives about 0.6 V onto its D+ and reads D-. Below the 0.3 V data-detect level it sees a standard downstream port; between 0.3 V and 0.8 V it sees a charging port. In secondary detection it drives about 0.6 V onto D- and reads D+: below 0.3 V means a charging downstream port, between 0.3 V and 0.8 V a dedicated charging port."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Auto-detect",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Each port has its own auto-detect block watching its data lines. A divider-type device gets 2.7 V on DP and 2.7 V on DM. A BC1.2 device makes the controller switch DP and DM into the shorted mode. A device built for the 1.2 V / 1.2 V scheme gets 1.2 V on both lines. Inside, switches S1 and S2 connect the two 2.7 V sources, S3 the 1.2 V source and S4 the DP-DM short: Divider with S1 and S2 on and S3, S4 off; short with S4 on alone; 1.2 V with S3 and S4 on and S1, S2 off. Port 2 of the TPS2513A-Q1 is built the same way. In short mode, if DP falls below V(DPL_TH_DETACH), 330 mV typ, the port returns to divider mode."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shorted mode",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "BC1.2 and YD/T 1591-2009 ask for D+ and D- tied together through no more than 200 Ω."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Divider mode",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "There are three divider schemes. Divider 1, for 5 W adapters, puts 2 V on D+ and 2.7 V on D-. Divider 2, for 10 W adapters, puts 2.7 V on D+ and 2 V on D-. Divider 3, for 12 W adapters, puts 2.7 V on both. Only Divider 3 is built into these devices; it supports a charging current up to 2.4 A."
  },
  {
   "topic": "1.2 V mode",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "Some tablet chargers hold the shorted data lines at 1.2 V, with at most 200 Ω between D+ and D-."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Undervoltage lockout",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Until IN rises past the UVLO threshold (4.1 V typ) the DP and DM outputs are held off. The threshold has hysteresis (100 mV typ) so a supply dip from a current surge does not make the outputs chatter."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power on and power off",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "At power-up the data lines step from 0 V to about 2.7 V while VIN is still climbing past about 4.2 V. At power-down the data lines drop to 0 V when VIN has fallen to about 4.0 V, while VIN carries on discharging."
  },
  {
   "topic": "VBUS",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "The controllers only set the data-line signature; they do not deliver VBUS power. Where the port needs overcurrent protection a USB power switch is used: TPS2561A-Q1 (dual) with the TPS2513A-Q1, TPS2557-Q1 (single) with the TPS2514A-Q1. With the TPS2561A-Q1, its current limit (set by RILIM) is to stay above 2.4 A per channel."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Port types in BC1.2",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "BC1.2 names three downstream port types: SDP (standard downstream port), CDP (charging downstream port) and DCP (dedicated charging port). A charging port is a downstream USB port that supplies power to charge portable equipment."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 14,
   "text": "Put a ceramic bypass capacitor of 0.1 µF or more from IN to GND as near the device as possible. When a USB power switch is used, join the controller's IN to the switch's IN. DP1/DM1 serve one port and DP2/DM2 the other; route each DP and DM pair as a matched pair. The example layout puts the controller beside the TPS2561A-Q1 with a high-frequency bypass capacitor at the switch input and vias to a power ground plane."
  }
 ],
